REV.page({"course":"python","id":"w01","title":"Variables, data types, operators and strings","summary":"Your first Python programs. Printing and reading input, storing values in variables, the five basic data types and how to convert between them, the three families of operators, and the basics of strings — indexing, slicing, joining, repeating and comparing.","n":1,"lectures":[{"code":"1.1","title":"Introduction to programming basics in python","video":"8ndsDXohLMQ","seconds":362},{"code":"1.2","title":"Introduction to REPLIT","video":"NgZZ0HIUqbs","seconds":502},{"code":"1.3","title":"More on REPLIT, print command & common python mistakes","video":"As7_aq6XGfI","seconds":617},{"code":"1.4","title":"A quick introduction to variables","video":"Yg6xzi2ie5s","seconds":376},{"code":"1.5","title":"Variables & input statement","video":"ruQb8jzkGyQ","seconds":495},{"code":"1.6","title":"Variables & literals in python","video":"tDaXdoKfX0k","seconds":558},{"code":"1.7","title":"Data types part 1","video":"8n4MBjuDBu4","seconds":682},{"code":"1.8","title":"Data types part 2","video":"xQXxufhEJHw","seconds":594},{"code":"1.9","title":"Operators & expressions | part 1","video":"8pu73HKzNOE","seconds":542},{"code":"1.10","title":"Operators & expressions | part 2","video":"Y53K9FFu97Q","seconds":727},{"code":"1.11","title":"Introduction to strings & string slicing in python","video":"sS89tiDuqoM","seconds":585},{"code":"1.12","title":"More on strings","video":"e45MVXwya7A","seconds":825},{"code":"1.13","title":"Conclusion | FAQs & tips for beginner python programmers","video":"_Ccezy5hlc8","seconds":612}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"why-programming-and-why-python","level":2,"title":"Why programming, and why Python"},{"id":"replit-where-you-type-and-run-code","level":2,"title":"Replit: where you type and run code"},{"id":"the-print-command","level":2,"title":"The print command"},{"id":"a-staircase-of-stars","level":3,"title":"A staircase of stars"},{"id":"several-values-in-one-print","level":3,"title":"Several values in one print"},{"id":"syntax-and-error-messages","level":2,"title":"Syntax and error messages"},{"id":"how-to-read-an-error-message","level":3,"title":"How to read an error message"},{"id":"variables","level":2,"title":"Variables"},{"id":"a-a-1-changing-a-variable","level":3,"title":"a=a+1: changing a variable"},{"id":"reading-input-from-the-user","level":2,"title":"Reading input from the user"},{"id":"names-and-places","level":3,"title":"Names and places"},{"id":"the-message-inside-input","level":3,"title":"The message inside input"},{"id":"variables-and-literals","level":2,"title":"Variables and literals"},{"id":"when-to-use-a-variable-and-when-a-literal","level":3,"title":"When to use a variable, and when a literal"},{"id":"data-types","level":2,"title":"Data types"},{"id":"lists","level":3,"title":"Lists"},{"id":"booleans-and-type-conversion","level":2,"title":"Booleans and type conversion"},{"id":"to-an-integer-int","level":3,"title":"To an integer: int()"},{"id":"to-a-float-float","level":3,"title":"To a float: float()"},{"id":"to-a-string-str","level":3,"title":"To a string: str()"},{"id":"to-a-boolean-bool","level":3,"title":"To a Boolean: bool()"},{"id":"operators-and-expressions","level":2,"title":"Operators and expressions"},{"id":"numbers","level":3,"title":"Numbers"},{"id":"the-data-type-decides-what-an-operator-does","level":3,"title":"The data type decides what an operator does"},{"id":"division-gives-a-float","level":3,"title":"Division gives a float"},{"id":"operator-precedence","level":3,"title":"Operator precedence"},{"id":"arithmetic-relational-and-logical-operators","level":2,"title":"Arithmetic, relational and logical operators"},{"id":"arithmetic-operators","level":3,"title":"Arithmetic operators"},{"id":"relational-operators","level":3,"title":"Relational operators"},{"id":"logical-operators","level":3,"title":"Logical operators"},{"id":"strings-indexing-slicing-and-joining","level":2,"title":"Strings: indexing, slicing and joining"},{"id":"indexing-one-character","level":3,"title":"Indexing: one character"},{"id":"slicing-a-piece-of-a-string","level":3,"title":"Slicing: a piece of a string"},{"id":"digits-in-a-string-are-characters","level":3,"title":"Digits in a string are characters"},{"id":"more-on-strings","level":2,"title":"More on strings"},{"id":"replication","level":3,"title":"Replication"},{"id":"comparing-strings","level":3,"title":"Comparing strings"},{"id":"negative-indexing","level":3,"title":"Negative indexing"},{"id":"index-out-of-range-and-len","level":3,"title":"Index out of range, and len()"},{"id":"tips-for-beginners","level":2,"title":"Tips for beginners"},{"id":"error-messages-of-this-week","level":2,"title":"Error messages of this week"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Programming</strong> means giving a computer exact instructions in a language it understands. The course uses <strong>Python</strong>: easy to learn, powerful and very widely used.</li>\n<li><code>print(...)</code> shows values in the console. Several values separated by commas are printed on one line, with one space between them.</li>\n<li>A <strong>variable</strong> is a name for a stored value. <code>a=10</code> stores 10 in <code>a</code>; <code>a=a+1</code> takes the old value, adds 1 and stores the result back.</li>\n<li><code>input()</code> waits for the user and returns what they typed as a <strong>string</strong> (text). <code>int(input())</code> turns it into a whole number.</li>\n<li>Every value has a <strong>data type</strong>: <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>bool</code> or <code>list</code>. <code>type(x)</code> shows it; <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, <code>str()</code> and <code>bool()</code> convert between types.</li>\n<li><strong>Operators</strong>: arithmetic (<code>+ - * / // % **</code>), relational (<code>&gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;= == !=</code>, which give <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>) and logical (<code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>). <code>*</code> is done before <code>+</code> unless brackets say otherwise.</li>\n<li><strong>Strings</strong>: <code>s[0]</code> is the first character and <code><span>s[-1]</span></code> the last; <code>s[1:3]</code> is a slice; <code>+</code> joins, <code>*</code> repeats, <code>len(s)</code> counts the characters; comparison goes character by character.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors are normal.</strong> The last line of an error message names the error; the line number tells you where to look.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-programming-and-why-python\">Why programming, and why Python</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To talk to people in Germany, you learn German; in France, French. To talk to a <strong>computer</strong>, you also need a language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). A <strong>programming language</strong> is a language that a computer understands. A <strong>program</strong> is a list of instructions written in it, and <strong>programming</strong> (or <strong>coding</strong>) is writing those instructions.</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>There are many programming languages, just as India has many spoken languages. The course teaches <strong>Python</strong> for three reasons: it is very widely used, it is easy to learn, and it is powerful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Learning to code has become much easier with time. With Pascal, COBOL, C or C++ in the 1980s and 1990s, it took weeks of practice before you could write useful code; with Python you can start within hours (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Programming is fun because you make the computer do seemingly complex things, and do them really fast (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The course assumes no computer-science knowledge, only basic English.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learn it like driving</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Books and videos alone do not teach you to drive a car. You learn when you hold the steering (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Programming is the same: keep an editor open and type the code along with the lecturers, Prof. Sudarshan Iyengar and Omkar. In Week 1 every program is short, at most about half a page (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ndsDXohLMQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"replit-where-you-type-and-run-code\">Replit: where you type and run code</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To run a program you need two things: a place to type it, and a way to make the computer carry it out. <strong>Replit</strong> (replit.com) gives you both inside the web browser, so you do not need to install anything (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Getting started, as in the lecture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open replit.com and click <strong>Start coding</strong>.</li>\n<li>Log in, for example with your Google account.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>+</strong>, choose <strong>Python</strong>, replace the random name with your own (the lecture uses “firstcode”) and click <strong>Create repl</strong>. A <em>repl</em> is just a place where you write and run code.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Editor, console and Run</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>editor</strong> is where you type your commands; together they form the program, saved in a file such as <code>main.py</code>. The <strong>console</strong> is where the computer shows the <strong>output</strong>, that is, the result (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). The <strong>Run</strong> button runs the program: the computer carries out the commands one at a time, from the first line to the last.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The left panel of Replit has more features (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Files</strong>: <em>Add file</em> makes a new program file (the lecture makes <code>newprog.py</code>; Python files end in <code>.py</code>), and <em>Add folder</em> makes a folder (the lecture makes <code>week 1</code>). Drag files into folders to keep your programs organised.</li>\n<li>Icons for version control, packages, secrets, databases and unit tests, which the course does not need yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Settings</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>): the <em>layout</em> can be side by side (editor left, console right) or stacked (editor on top, console below); the <em>theme</em> can be light or dark; and you can change the <em>font size</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Code intelligence</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>): when it is enabled, Replit suggests commands while you type and shows help about them, for example about <code>print</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The screens change</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The videos were recorded in 2021, and Replit looks different today. The idea is the same in every Python editor: a place to type, a Run button and an area for the output. The Replit in the videos runs <strong>Python 3.8</strong>; this matters for the wording of some error messages below. If Replit says it “failed to connect”, your internet connection dropped: wait a moment and run again (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-print-command\">The print command</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>print</code> tells the computer to show something in the console. Whatever you put inside its round brackets is shown.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The first program of the course (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=105s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:45</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hello\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>print</code> is the command. Python calls such named commands <strong>functions</strong>; this week you also meet <code>input</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>bool</code> and <code>len</code>.</li>\n<li>The round brackets <code>( )</code> hold what you want to print.</li>\n<li><code>&quot;Hello&quot;</code> is text. Text inside quotes is called a <strong>string</strong>. The quotes tell Python where the text starts and ends; they are not printed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each <code>print</code> prints one line, so two <code>print</code> commands give two lines (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Namasthey India&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;This is the second line&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Namasthey India\nThis is the second line\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"a-staircase-of-stars\">A staircase of stars</h3>\n<p>The lecture prints a pattern, one line per command (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;**&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;***&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>*\n**\n***\n</code></pre>\n<p>It continues the same way up to eight lines. On the eighth line it types more stars than the pattern needs, and the console shows exactly those stars.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The computer does exactly what you type</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The computer does not know that you meant a pattern. It prints precisely what each command says, no more and no less (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Add a <code>#</code> after the stars and it prints the <code>#</code> too; <code>print(<wbr>&quot;I dont know what to type&quot;)</code> prints just that.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The staircase the other way round needs spaces in front of the stars (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:50</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;  *&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot; **&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;***&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>  *\n **\n***\n</code></pre>\n<p>For four lines, every line needs one more space in front (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;   *&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;  **&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot; ***&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;****&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>   *\n  **\n ***\n****\n</code></pre>\n<p>So each new line means editing all the lines above it; for 20 lines this is hard work. The lecture asks: <em>can we automate this?</em> Yes: loops, in a later week, print such patterns in three or four lines (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NgZZ0HIUqbs&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"several-values-in-one-print\">Several values in one print</h3>\n<p><code>print</code> can print several values at once. Separate them with commas (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hello India&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hello World&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hi Python students&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hello India Hello World Hi Python students\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The values are printed in the order you write them, on one line (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><code>print</code> puts <strong>one space</strong> between the values. The help text that code intelligence shows for <code>print</code> says the same: the separator between values is “a space” by default, and a new line comes after the last value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Numbers can be printed too. Write them <strong>without</strong> quotes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20.5</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hello India&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20.5</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>10\n20.5\nHello India 10 20.5\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>10</code> is a whole number and <code>20.5</code> is a fractional number (it has a decimal point). One <code>print</code> can mix strings, whole numbers and fractional numbers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"syntax-and-error-messages\">Syntax and error messages</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syntax</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>syntax</strong> of a language is its set of rules for how code must be written (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). If code breaks a rule, Python does not run it; it shows an <strong>error message</strong> instead.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture tests the rules on <code>print('Hi')</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). The results in the video (Python 3.8):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You type</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print('Hi')</code></td>\n<td><code>Hi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print['Hi']</code></td>\n<td><code>TypeError: 'builtin_<wbr>function_<wbr>or_<wbr>method' object is not subscriptable</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print{'Hi'}</code></td>\n<td><code>SyntaxError: invalid syntax</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print&lt;'Hi'&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>SyntaxError: invalid syntax</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print(Hi)</code></td>\n<td><code>NameError: name 'Hi' is not defined</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>print(&quot;Hi&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>Hi</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The four rules for print</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Spell <code>print</code> correctly, in small letters.</li>\n<li>Put round brackets <code>( )</code> after it. Square <code>[ ]</code>, curly <code>{ }</code> and angle <code>&lt; &gt;</code> brackets do not work: Python is “very strict” about brackets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Put text inside quotes. Without quotes, Python reads <code>Hi</code> as the name of a variable, finds none, and gives <code>NameError</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Single quotes <code>'Hi'</code> and double quotes <code>&quot;Hi&quot;</code> both work: Python is “very flexible” about quotes. But a string must end with the <strong>same</strong> kind of quote it starts with (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=As7_aq6XGfI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture only says that mixed quotes fail. This is what Python 3.12 prints for them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hi&#x27;)\n</span></code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>SyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 1)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python finds no closing <code>&quot;</code>, so for Python the string never ends: it is “unterminated”. Newer Python also words one of the table’s messages differently: for <code>print{'Hi'}</code>, Python 3.12 says <code>SyntaxError: Missing parentheses in call to 'print'. Did you mean print(...)?</code></p>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-read-an-error-message\">How to read an error message</h3>\n<p>The lecturers do not edit their mistakes out of the videos, so that you see how to fix them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). Here is one from lecture 1.5, exactly as the console showed it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 5, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(&quot;Hello&quot;,n,&quot;How is the weather in&quot;,p)\nNameError: name 'p' is not defined\n</code></pre>\n<p>Read it from the bottom up:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The last line</strong> names the <strong>type</strong> of error (<code>NameError</code>) and explains it: the name <code>p</code> is not defined, that is, no value was ever stored in <code>p</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The line above it</strong> shows the code that failed.</li>\n<li><strong><code>line 5</code></strong> tells you where that code is in your file.</li>\n<li><code>Traceback (most recent call last)</code> only means “here is where Python was when it stopped”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Python 3.11 and later also draw <code>^</code> marks under the exact spot.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two kinds of error</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>syntax error</strong> is found <strong>before</strong> the program runs: Python checks the whole file first, so nothing at all is printed, and the message starts with <code>File …</code>. The other errors (<code>NameError</code>, <code>TypeError</code>, <code>ValueError</code>, <code>IndexError</code>) happen <strong>while</strong> the program runs: the lines before the bad line have already run and printed their output, and the message starts with <code>Traceback</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The line number can point after the mistake</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In lecture 1.7 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>) line 7 was <code>print(<wbr>type(n)</code>, with its last <code>)</code> missing. Python 3.8 reported <code>SyntaxError: invalid syntax</code> at <strong>line 8</strong>, the line after the mistake, because it noticed the problem only when the next line began. Python 3.10 and later point at the right line and say <code>SyntaxError: '(<wbr>' was never closed</code>. So when the reported line looks correct, check the line above it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"variables\">Variables</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In mathematics you write “let <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span>”</span> and then use <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span></span> instead of 10. Python lets you do the same: give a value a <strong>name</strong>, then use the name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Variable and assignment</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a name that refers to a stored value. Think of a labelled box or a bucket that holds one value. The line <code>a=10</code> is an <strong>assignment</strong>: it stores the value on the right of <code>=</code> (here 10) in the variable on the left (<code>a</code>). Read <code>=</code> as “gets” or “becomes”, not as “is equal to”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s program (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\nb=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a+b)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a*b)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>10\n20\n30\n200\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li><code>a=10</code> stores 10 in <code>a</code>. Nothing is printed.</li>\n<li><code>print(a)</code> has no quotes, so Python prints the <strong>value</strong> of <code>a</code>: 10.</li>\n<li><code>b=20</code> stores 20 in <code>b</code>, and <code>print(b)</code> prints 20.</li>\n<li><code>print(a+b)</code>: Python first works out <code>a+b</code> = 10 + 20 = 30, then prints 30.</li>\n<li><code>print(a*b)</code>: <code>*</code> means multiply (“a into b”), so 10 × 20 = 200 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Spaces around = do not matter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>a=10</code> and <code>a = 10</code> are the same to Python, and so are <code>a+b</code> and <code>a + b</code>. Prof. Sudarshan types without spaces; Omkar puts spaces around <code>=</code> and after commas. Spaces <strong>inside quotes</strong> do matter: they are part of the text, as the reverse staircase showed.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-a-1-changing-a-variable\"><code>a=a+1</code>: changing a variable</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\na=a+<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\na=a+<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\na=a+<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>10\n11\n12\n13\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python carries out an assignment in two steps: first it works out the right side with the <strong>current</strong> values, then it stores the result in the variable on the left (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line</th>\n<th>Code</th>\n<th><code>a</code> after the line</th>\n<th>Printed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>a=10</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>print(a)</code></td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><code>10</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><code>a=a+1</code></td>\n<td>10 + 1 = 11</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><code>print(a)</code></td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><code>11</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><code>a=a+1</code></td>\n<td>11 + 1 = 12</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><code>print(a)</code></td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><code>12</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><code>a=a+1</code></td>\n<td>12 + 1 = 13</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><code>print(a)</code></td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><code>13</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A variable keeps its value until another assignment changes it: printing <code>a</code> twice in a row shows the same value twice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Increment</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Adding 1 to a variable, as in <code>a=a+1</code>, is called <strong>incrementing</strong> it. In real life too, to increment is to go one step up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>a=a+1</code> is not an equation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In algebra <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> is impossible: subtract <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span></span> from both sides and you get <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>.</span> In Python it is not a statement of fact but an <strong>instruction</strong>: “take the value of <code>a</code>, add 1, and store the result back in <code>a</code>”. It is one of the most frequently used lines in programming (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"reading-input-from-the-user\">Reading input from the user</h2>\n<p>So far the values were written inside the program. A program becomes <strong>interactive</strong> when the user can type values while it runs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Enter a number:&quot;</span>)\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n+<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n+<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n+<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<p>When the user types <code>100</code> and presses Enter, the console shows:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Enter a number:\n100\n100\n101\n102\n103\n</code></pre>\n<ol>\n<li>Line 1 prints the message <code>Enter a number:</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 2 does three things, from the inside out (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>):\n<ul>\n<li><code>input()</code> stops the program and waits; the cursor blinks in the console. The user types <code>100</code> and presses Enter, and <code>input()</code> gives back what was typed as a <strong>string</strong>: the text <code>'100'</code>.</li>\n<li><code>int(...)</code> converts that text into the integer 100.</li>\n<li><code>n=</code> stores 100 in <code>n</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Line 3 prints 100.</li>\n<li>Lines 4 to 6 print 100 + 1 = 101, 100 + 2 = 102 and 100 + 3 = 103 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yg6xzi2ie5s&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The first <code>100</code> in the output is what the user typed; the second is printed by line 3.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Integer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>integer</strong> is a whole number with no decimal part, such as 100, 0 or −7. Python calls this type <code>int</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">If the user types something that is not a whole number</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>int()</code> can convert only text that looks like a whole number. If the user types <code>abc</code> or <code>5.3</code>, the program stops with an error (not shown in the lecture):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'\n</code></pre>\n<p>The message means: <code>'abc'</code> is not a whole number written in ordinary decimal digits (“base 10”).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"names-and-places\">Names and places</h3>\n<p>Lecture 1.5 asks for a name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello, type in your name:&quot;</span>)\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>,n,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;how are you?&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Typing <code>Sudarshan</code> gives (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hello, type in your name:\nSudarshan\nHello Sudarshan how are you?\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A name is text, not a number, so the lecture writes <code>str(input())</code>; <code>str</code> stands for <strong>string</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In <code>print(<wbr>&quot;Hello&quot;,<wbr>n,<wbr>&quot;how are you?&quot;)</code> the quoted parts are printed as they are, and <code>n</code> (no quotes) is replaced by its value.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>str(input())</code> and <code>input()</code> give the same thing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>input()</code> always returns a string, so wrapping it in <code>str(...)</code> changes nothing. The lecture writes <code>str(...)</code> to make clear that a string is expected, next to <code>int(...)</code> for numbers. Both forms are correct.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Next the program also asks for the place. The lecture’s first try has a mistake in line 4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello, type in your name:&quot;</span>)\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Which place are you in?&quot;</span>)\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>,n,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;How is the weather in&quot;</span>,p)\n</code></pre>\n<p>After the user types <code>Sudarshan</code> and <code>Mysore</code>, line 5 stops with the <code>NameError</code> shown in the previous section: line 4 stored the place in <code>n</code>, so <code>p</code> was never given a value. The fix is <code>p=<wbr>str(<wbr>input())</code> in line 4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">One variable holds one value</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Storing the place in <code>n</code> also <strong>loses the name</strong>: the new value replaces the old one in the bucket (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). Use a new variable for each value you need to keep.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The final program adds the age (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello, type in your name:&quot;</span>)\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Which place are you in?&quot;</span>)\np=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>,n,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;How is the weather in&quot;</span>,p,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;?&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What is your age?&quot;</span>)\nage=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>())\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Good to know you are&quot;</span>,age,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;years old&quot;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Hello, type in your name:\nSudarshan\nWhich place are you in?\nMysore\nHello Sudarshan How is the weather in Mysore ?\nWhat is your age?\n15\nGood to know you are 15 years old\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The age is a number, so it is read with <code>int(input())</code>.</li>\n<li>A variable name can be a whole word, such as <code>age</code>, not just one letter.</li>\n<li>The program works for any person: run it again with <code>Amitabh</code> and <code>Mumbai</code> and it greets Amitabh (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Notice the space in <code>Mysore ?</code>: <code>print</code> puts a space before the <code>?</code> because <code>&quot;?&quot;</code> is a separate value. The lecture leaves removing this space as a puzzle to look up online, and mentions <em>format specifiers</em> as one tool for it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruQb8jzkGyQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-message-inside-input\">The message inside input</h3>\n<p>Lecture 1.6 starts from the same program, with Omkar’s messages (<code>'Type your name: '</code>, <code>'Type your location: '</code>), and shows that <code>print</code> and <code>input</code> can be merged: put the message inside the brackets of <code>input</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">n = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Type your name: &#x27;</span>))\np = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Type your location: &#x27;</span>))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hello&#x27;</span>, n, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;How is the weather in&#x27;</span>, p, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;?&#x27;</span>)\nage = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;What is your age?&#x27;</span>))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Good to know that you are&#x27;</span>, age, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;years old&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Type your name: Sudarshan\nType your location: Mysore\nHello Sudarshan How is the weather in Mysore ?\nWhat is your age?40\nGood to know that you are 40 years old\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>input(<wbr>'Type your name: ')</code> prints the message and waits <strong>on the same line</strong>, so the user’s answer appears right after it. Such a message is called a <strong>prompt</strong>.</li>\n<li><code>What is your <span>age?40</span></code> has no space because the prompt <code>'What is your age?'</code> does not end with a space. The other two prompts end with a space, so their answers are neatly separated.</li>\n<li>The same program works when the values change: with <code>Omkar</code>, <code>Pune</code> and <code>30</code> it prints <code>Hello Omkar How is the weather in Pune ?</code> and <code>Good to know that you are 30 years old</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"variables-and-literals\">Variables and literals</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Literal</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>literal</strong> is a value written directly in the code, such as <code>10</code>, <code>3.14</code>, <code>'Sudarshan'</code> or <code>'Mysore'</code>. A <strong>variable</strong> is a container whose value can change while the program runs; the literals are the actual values stored in it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s illustration (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">name = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Sudarshan&#x27;</span>\nname = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Omkar&#x27;</span>\nname = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Sudarshan&#x27;</span>\nage = <span class=\"hljs-number\">40</span>\nage = <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>\nage = age + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span> = <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>name</code> and <code>age</code> are variables. Their values change: <code>name</code> holds <code>'Sudarshan'</code>, then <code>'Omkar'</code>, then <code>'Sudarshan'</code> again; <code>age</code> holds 40, then 30.</li>\n<li><code>age = age + 1</code> works: take the latest value of <code>age</code> (30), add 1, and store 31 back in <code>age</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li><code>30 = 30 + 1</code> does <strong>not</strong> work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). It asks Python to store 31 <em>in</em> the number 30, and a number is not a container. Python 3.12 stops with the message below; Python 3.8 says just <code>SyntaxError: cannot assign to literal</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>SyntaxError: cannot assign to literal here. Maybe you meant '==' instead of '='?\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The rule</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>variable</strong> may stand on either side of <code>=</code>. A <strong>literal</strong> may stand only on the right side (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-use-a-variable-and-when-a-literal\">When to use a variable, and when a literal</h3>\n<p>The lecture answers with a program for the area of a circle, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">π</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">r = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">input</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Enter the radius of the circle: &#x27;</span>))\narea = <span class=\"hljs-number\">3.14</span> * r * r\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Area of the circle with radius&#x27;</span>, r, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;is&#x27;</span>, area)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Enter the radius of the circle: 5\nArea of the circle with radius 5 is 78.5\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Radius 5, then radius 15 (the lecture’s runs at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Line 1 reads the text <code>'5'</code>, converts it to the integer 5 and stores it in <code>r</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 2 works from left to right: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3.14</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15.7</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Then <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15.7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">78.5</span></span></span></span>,</span> which is stored in <code>area</code>.</li>\n<li>Line 3 prints the four values with a space between each.</li>\n<li>Run again with radius 15: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3.14</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">47.1</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> then <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">47.1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">706.5</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the area is 78.5 for radius 5, and 706.5 for radius 15.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rule of thumb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Use a <strong>variable</strong> when the value may change. The radius changes from run to run and the area changes with it, so <code>r</code> and <code>area</code> are variables; so are a name and an age. Use a <strong>literal</strong> when the value is fixed: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">π</span></span></span></span></span> is always 3.14, so it is written as the literal <code>3.14</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tDaXdoKfX0k&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"data-types\">Data types</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span><span class=\"box-t\">Jars in a kitchen</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>At home, rice is kept in a jar that keeps out moisture, and water in a bottle. Different things need different containers, and not just for looks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). A computer is similar: it stores different kinds of data in different ways in its memory. The kind of a value is called its <strong>data type</strong>, and Python works out the type by itself from the way you write the value.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Data type</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>data type</strong> of a value says what kind of value it is, and so how Python stores it and which operations work on it. Week 1 meets five types:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Name and meaning</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>int</code></td>\n<td>integer: a whole number</td>\n<td><code>10</code>, <code>0</code>, <code><span>-10</span></code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>float</code></td>\n<td>floating-point number: has a decimal point</td>\n<td><code>6.3</code>, <code>5.6</code>, <code>20.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>str</code></td>\n<td>string: text in quotes</td>\n<td><code>'sudarshan'</code>, <code>'India'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bool</code></td>\n<td>Boolean: only two values</td>\n<td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>list</code></td>\n<td>list: several values in square brackets</td>\n<td><code>[10,20,30]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<p><code>type(x)</code> tells you the data type of <code>x</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">n=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\nr=<span class=\"hljs-number\">6.3</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(r)\ns=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sudarshan&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;n is of type:&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(n))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;r is of type:&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(r))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s is of type:&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(s))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>10\n6.3\nsudarshan\nn is of type: &lt;class 'int'&gt;\nr is of type: &lt;class 'float'&gt;\ns is of type: &lt;class 'str'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The output <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code> looks strange (“very ugly”, says the lecture); just read the word inside the quotes. It says that <code>n</code> holds an <code>int</code>.</li>\n<li>Nobody told Python these types; it decided by itself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). <code>10</code> has no decimal part, so <code>int</code>; <code>6.3</code> has one, so <code>float</code>; <code>'sudarshan'</code> is in quotes, so <code>str</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Float</strong> is short for <em>floating-point number</em>, the way a computer stores a number that is not an integer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quotes change the meaning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <code>print(<wbr>&quot;n is of type:&quot;,<wbr>type(n))</code> the first <code>n</code> is inside quotes, so the <strong>letter</strong> n is printed. The second <code>n</code> has no quotes, so Python uses the <strong>variable</strong> <code>n</code> and its value 10 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"lists\">Lists</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">List and index</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>list</strong> holds several values in order. Write them inside square brackets, separated by commas: <code><span>l=[10,20,30]</span></code>. Each value has a position number called its <strong>index</strong>. Python starts counting at <strong>0</strong>: the first value is <code>l[0]</code>, the second is <code>l[1]</code>, and so on (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><code>print(l)</code> prints the whole list as <code>[10, 20, 30]</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). Then the lecture adds two more numbers and prints the values one by one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">l=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">68</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">732</span>]\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>])\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>])\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>])\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>10\n20\n30\n68\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>0</th>\n<th>1</th>\n<th>2</th>\n<th>3</th>\n<th>4</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Value</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>68</td>\n<td>732</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Humans count 1, 2, 3; a computer usually counts 0, 1, 2. So <code>l[3]</code> is the <strong>fourth</strong> value, 68.</li>\n<li><code>print(<wbr>type(l))</code> prints <code>&lt;class 'list'&gt;</code>: a list is a data type of its own (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). Lists come back in detail in later weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s revision run (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>), after fixing the missing <code>)</code> described in the warning above:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">l=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">68</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">732</span>]\nn=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\nr=<span class=\"hljs-number\">6.9</span>\ns=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;India&#x27;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(l))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(n))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(r))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(s))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>&lt;class 'list'&gt;\n&lt;class 'int'&gt;\n&lt;class 'float'&gt;\n&lt;class 'str'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"booleans-and-type-conversion\">Booleans and type conversion</h2>\n<p>Lecture 1.8 repeats <code>type</code> with <code>i = 10</code>, <code>f = 5.6</code> and <code>s = 'India'</code> (an <code>int</code>, a <code>float</code> and a <code>str</code>), then adds a new type (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Boolean</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>Boolean</strong> (type <code>bool</code>) has only two possible values: <code>True</code> and <code>False</code>. The first letter must be a capital: <code>True</code>, not <code>true</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">i = <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>\nf = <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.6</span>\ns = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;India&#x27;</span>\nb1 = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>\nb2 = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(i))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(f))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(s))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b1))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b2))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;\n&lt;class 'float'&gt;\n&lt;class 'str'&gt;\n&lt;class 'bool'&gt;\n&lt;class 'bool'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>true</code> is not a Boolean</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With a small first letter, Python reads <code>true</code> as the name of a variable. <code>b1 = true</code> stops with <code>NameError: name 'true' is not defined</code> (Python 3.12 adds <code>Did you mean: 'True'?</code>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Type conversion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Type conversion</strong>, also called <strong>type casting</strong>, makes a value of one type from a value of another type (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). Write the name of the new type and put the value in brackets: <code>int(...)</code>, <code>float(...)</code>, <code>str(...)</code> or <code>bool(...)</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"to-an-integer-int\">To an integer: <code>int()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5.7</span>)\nb = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;10&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(a))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>5 &lt;class 'int'&gt;\n10 &lt;class 'int'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>int(5.7)</code> keeps only the whole-number part, 5. The <code>.7</code> is dropped, not rounded, so the answer is 5, not 6 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li><code>'10'</code> is in quotes, so it is a string; <code>int('10')</code> turns it into the number 10.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"to-a-float-float\">To a float: <code>float()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">float</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>)\nb = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">float</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;5.3&#x27;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(a))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>9.0 &lt;class 'float'&gt;\n5.3 &lt;class 'float'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The integer 9 becomes 9.0 (a float always shows a decimal point), and the string <code>'5.3'</code> becomes the number 5.3 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"to-a-string-str\">To a string: <code>str()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>)\nb = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5.3</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(a))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>9 &lt;class 'str'&gt;\n5.3 &lt;class 'str'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The printed values look the same as numbers, but they are now <strong>text</strong>: <code>'9'</code> and <code>'5.3'</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Text is not used for arithmetic: below you will see that <code>'4'+'7'</code> is <code>'47'</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"to-a-boolean-bool\">To a Boolean: <code>bool()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\nb = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nc = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">bool</span>(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(a))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(b))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(c, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(c))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>True &lt;class 'bool'&gt;\nFalse &lt;class 'bool'&gt;\nTrue &lt;class 'bool'&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture repeats this with floats (<code>10.5</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code><span>-10.4</span></code>) and with strings (<code>'India'</code>, <code>'10'</code>, <code><span>'-10.4'</span></code>, <code>'0'</code>, <code>''</code>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). All the results:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th><code>bool(value)</code></th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>10</code>, <code><span>-10</span></code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td>a number that is not zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>10.5</code>, <code><span>-10.4</span></code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td>a number that is not zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>0.0</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>0.0 is just zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'India'</code>, <code>'10'</code>, <code><span>'-10.4'</span></code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td>a string that is not empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>'0'</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td>in quotes, so a string of one character, not the number 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>''</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td>the <strong>empty string</strong>: quotes with nothing inside</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which values are False?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Only the “nothing” values: the number <code>0</code> (also written <code>0.0</code>) and the empty string <code>''</code>. Every other number, positive or negative, and every other string is <code>True</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xQXxufhEJHw&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"operators-and-expressions\">Operators and expressions</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Operator, operand and expression</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>operator</strong> is a symbol for an operation, such as <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code> (multiply) or <code>/</code> (divide) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). The values it works on are its <strong>operands</strong>. An <strong>expression</strong> is a combination of values, variables and operators that Python works out to one value, such as <code>3+2</code> or <code>a*b</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"numbers\">Numbers</h3>\n<p>The lecture prints <code>3+2</code> (5) and <code>3*2</code> (6), then this (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">n=<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>*<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.6</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>7.800000000000001\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecturer reads the answer as 7.8, but the console shows <code>7.800000000000001</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why 7.800000000000001?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A computer stores a float in binary (base 2). Most decimal fractions, such as 2.6, cannot be stored exactly in binary, just as <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span> cannot be written exactly as a decimal <span class=\"nw\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.333</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span></span></span></span>).</span> The stored 2.6 is a tiny bit off, so the product is a tiny bit off too: the error is in the 15th decimal place. This happens with floats in every programming language; it is not a mistake in your code.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>With variables it works the same way: <code>a=1</code>, <code>b=2</code> and <code>n=a+b</code> give 3, and with <code>a=1.1</code> instead the sum is 3.1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-data-type-decides-what-an-operator-does\">The data type decides what an operator does</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sudarshan&#x27;</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;india&#x27;</span>\nn=a*b\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 3, in &lt;module&gt;\n    n=a*b\nTypeError: can't multiply sequence by non-int of type 'str'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Python does not know how to multiply two strings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The message calls a string a <em>sequence</em> (a series of characters) and complains that the other operand is not an integer, a “non-int”. (Lecture 1.12 shows that a string times an integer does work.)</p>\n<p>Now change <code>*</code> to <code>+</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sudarshan&#x27;</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;india&#x27;</span>\nn=a+b\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>sudarshanindia\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Concatenation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Concatenation</strong> means joining strings one after the other with <code>+</code>. No space is added: <code>'sudarshan'+'india'</code> gives <code>'sudarshanindia'</code>. Python uses <code>+</code> for this by choice, for convenience; <code>-</code> between two strings is not defined (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><code>+</code> also joins two lists into one new list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>]\nb=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>]\nn=a+b\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>[1, 2, 3, 7, 9, 15]\n</code></pre>\n<p>With <code><span>b=[2,7,9,15]</span></code> the result is <code>[1, 2, 3, 2, 7, 9, 15]</code>: the 2 appears twice. So this is <strong>not</strong> the union of sets from school, where repeats are removed; <code>+</code> simply puts the lists one after the other (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same operator, different work</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>+</code> adds numbers, joins strings and joins lists. What it does depends on the data types of its operands (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"division-gives-a-float\">Division gives a float</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">a=<span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>\nb=<span class=\"hljs-number\">15</span>\nn=a/b\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>0.7333333333333333\n</code></pre>\n<p>Even though 11 and 15 are integers, <code>/</code> gives a float (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"operator-precedence\">Operator precedence</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">n=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>+<span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>*<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#My guess is n will be 46.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#The expected answer was incorrect.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#The correct answer turns out to be 36,</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#that is due to what is called</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#the operator precedence.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>36\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Comment</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A line that starts with <code>#</code> is a <strong>comment</strong>. Python ignores it completely. Comments are notes for people who read the code; the lecture uses them to write down its guess and the explanation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). (The lecture’s second comment is one long line; it is split here to fit a phone.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why 36 and not 46 (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The guess, “from left to right”: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">13</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span></span></span></span>,</span> then <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">46</span></span></span></span>.</span> Python does <strong>not</strong> do this.</li>\n<li>Python gives <code>*</code> priority over <code>+</code>, so it finds the <code>*</code> first: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">13</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Then it adds: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">26</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>To get 46, use brackets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>): <code>n=<wbr>((10+13)*2)</code> works out <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">13</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span></span></span></span></span> first, then <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">23</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">46</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>10+13*2</code> is 36; <code>((10+13)*2)</code> is 46.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Operator precedence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Operator precedence</strong> is the rule that decides which operator is applied first when an expression has several (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). <code>*</code> and <code>/</code> come before <code>+</code> and <code>-</code>, as in school mathematics, and brackets come before everything.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">When in doubt, use brackets</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer does not find precedence very important, because brackets let you say exactly what you want (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8pu73HKzNOE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Extra brackets never hurt.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"arithmetic-relational-and-logical-operators\">Arithmetic, relational and logical operators</h2>\n<p>Python’s operators fall into three families (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>): <strong>arithmetic</strong>, <strong>relational</strong> and <strong>logical</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"arithmetic-operators\">Arithmetic operators</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s program (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#Arithmetic (+, -, *, /, //, %, **)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Addition&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Subtraction&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span> - <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Multiplication&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span> * <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Division&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span> / <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Floor division&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span> // <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Modulus&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span> % <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Exponentiation&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span> ** <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Addition 5\nSubtraction 8\nMultiplication 20\nDivision 2.3333333333333335\nFloor division 2\nModulus 1\nExponentiation 36\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Example and result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>+</code></td>\n<td>addition</td>\n<td><code>2 + 3</code> gives <code>5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-</code></td>\n<td>subtraction</td>\n<td><code>9 - 1</code> gives <code>8</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>*</code></td>\n<td>multiplication</td>\n<td><code>5 * 4</code> gives <code>20</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>/</code></td>\n<td>division</td>\n<td><code>7 / 3</code> gives <code>2.333…</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>//</code></td>\n<td>floor division</td>\n<td><code>7 // 3</code> gives <code>2</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>%</code></td>\n<td>modulus (remainder)</td>\n<td><code>7 % 3</code> gives <code>1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>**</code></td>\n<td>exponentiation (power)</td>\n<td><code>6 ** 2</code> gives <code>36</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Python prints <code>7 / 3</code> as <code>2.3333333333333335</code>. The last digit 5 is the same tiny float error as in <code>7.800000000000001</code>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">7 divided by 3, three ways (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>School division: 3 goes into 7 two times, because <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span></span></span></span></span> is not more than 7, but <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span></span> is.</li>\n<li>So the whole-number part of the answer (the <strong>quotient</strong>) is 2: <code>7 // 3</code> gives <code>2</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</li>\n<li>What is left over is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> (the <strong>remainder</strong>): <code>7 % 3</code> gives <code>1</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>The exact share is <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.333</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span></span></span></span>:</span></span> <code>7 / 3</code> gives <code>2.3333333333333335</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>7 / 3</code> is 2.333…, <code>7 // 3</code> is 2 and <code>7 % 3</code> is 1.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f1\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Floor division and remainder</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">q</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span></span> are positive integers, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">q</span></span></span></span></span> is <code>a // b</code> (the quotient, the whole-number part of <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>)</span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span></span></span></span></span> is <code>a % b</code> (the remainder). For the lecture’s numbers: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n</div></section>\n<p><code>**</code> means “to the power of”: <code>6 ** 2</code> is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"relational-operators\">Relational operators</h3>\n<p>A <strong>relational operator</strong> compares two values, and the answer is always a Boolean, <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). You met these operators in the Computational Thinking course (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Lecture’s examples</th>\n<th>Results</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt;</code></td>\n<td>greater than</td>\n<td><code>5 &gt; 10</code>, <code>10 &gt; 5</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code>, <code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;</code></td>\n<td>less than</td>\n<td><code>5 &lt; 10</code>, <code>10 &lt; 5</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&gt;=</code></td>\n<td>greater than or equal to</td>\n<td><code>5 &gt; 5</code>, <code>5 &gt;= 5</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code>, <code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;=</code></td>\n<td>less than or equal to</td>\n<td><code>5 &lt; 5</code>, <code>5 &lt;= 5</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code>, <code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>==</code></td>\n<td>equal to</td>\n<td><code>5 == 50</code>, <code>5 == 5</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code>, <code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>!=</code></td>\n<td>not equal to</td>\n<td><code>5 != 50</code>, <code>5 != 5</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each pair is a two-line program, for example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#Relational (&gt;, &lt;, &gt;=, &lt;=, ==, !=)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span> &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>False\nTrue\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>5 &gt; 5</code> is <code>False</code> because 5 is not greater than itself. <code>5 &gt;= 5</code> is <code>True</code>: “greater than <strong>or</strong> equal to” needs only one of the two parts to hold, and “equal” does.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>=</code> is not <code>==</code></span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>One <code>=</code> <strong>stores</strong> a value: <code>a=5</code>. Two <code>==</code> <strong>compare</strong>: <code>a==5</code> asks “is <code>a</code> equal to 5?” and gives <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Mixing them up is a very common mistake.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"logical-operators\">Logical operators</h3>\n<p>The <strong>logical operators</strong> <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code> work on Booleans (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\">#Logical (and, or, not)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">and</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">and</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">and</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">and</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>True\nFalse\nFalse\nFalse\n</code></pre>\n<p>With <code>or</code> in place of <code>and</code>, the same four lines print <code>True</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>True</code>, <code>False</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>A</th>\n<th>B</th>\n<th>A <code>and</code> B</th>\n<th>A <code>or</code> B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>True</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n<td><code>False</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code>and</code> gives <code>True</code> only when <strong>both</strong> operands are <code>True</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><code>or</code> gives <code>True</code> when <strong>at least one</strong> operand is <code>True</code>; it gives <code>False</code> only when both are <code>False</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">not</span> (<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>))\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">not</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>False\nTrue\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>not</code> flips a Boolean: <code>not True</code> is <code>False</code> and <code>not False</code> is <code>True</code>. The brackets are optional: <code>not (True)</code> and <code>not True</code> mean the same (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y53K9FFu97Q&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"strings-indexing-slicing-and-joining\">Strings: indexing, slicing and joining</h2>\n<p>A <strong>string</strong> is a sequence of characters. A <strong>character</strong> is one letter, digit, space or symbol. Lecture 1.11 starts with two strings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;coffee&#x27;</span>\nt=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;bread&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(t)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s+t)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>coffee\nbread\ncoffeebread\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>s+t</code> concatenates: <code>'coffee'</code> followed by <code>'bread'</code>, with no space between them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"indexing-one-character\">Indexing: one character</h3>\n<p>As in a list, each character of a string has an index, starting from 0 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Character</th>\n<th>c</th>\n<th>o</th>\n<th>f</th>\n<th>f</th>\n<th>e</th>\n<th>e</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Index</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So <code>s[0]</code> is <code>'c'</code>, <code>s[1]</code> is <code>'o'</code> and <code>s[2]</code> is <code>'f'</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"slicing-a-piece-of-a-string\">Slicing: a piece of a string</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;coffee&#x27;</span>\nt=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;bread&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>:<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>])\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>of\n</code></pre>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Slice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><code>s[a:b]</code> is the <strong>slice</strong> of <code>s</code> from index <code>a</code> up to <strong>but not including</strong> index <code>b</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). It contains the characters at indexes <code>a</code>, <code>a+1</code>, …, <code><span>b-1</span></code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f2\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Length of a slice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">len</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">])</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span></span></span></span></span> is a string and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7719em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span></span> are indexes from 0 to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">len</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>:</span> the slice starts at index <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span></span> and stops just before index <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>.</span> For <code>s[1:3]</code>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span></span> characters, <code>of</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><code>s[1:5]</code> for <code>s='coffee'</code> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Start at index 1: <code>o</code>.</li>\n<li>Stop <strong>before</strong> index 5, so the last index used is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Indexes 1, 2, 3 and 4 hold <code>o</code>, <code>f</code>, <code>f</code> and <code>e</code>.</li>\n<li>Length check: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span></span></span></span></span> characters.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>s[1:5]</code> is <code>offe</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>In the same way <code>s[3:5]</code> takes indexes 3 and 4 and prints <code>fe</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why start at 0, and why stop before b?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture admits it feels strange: why not go up to index 5 itself? It was the choice of Python’s designers, and there are good reasons for it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). The C language also starts counting at 0. Just remember: <strong>start included, stop excluded</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><code><span>s-t</span></code> is an error, because Python has no idea what subtracting strings means (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 3, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(s-t)\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for -: 'str' and 'str'\n</code></pre>\n<p>The last line says: the operator <code>-</code> does not support (cannot work on) two operands of type <code>str</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"digits-in-a-string-are-characters\">Digits in a string are characters</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s string of digits (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0123456789&#x27;</span>\na=s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>]\nb=s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a+b)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>4\n7\n47\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>In this string each digit sits at the index equal to itself, so <code>s[4]</code> is <code>'4'</code>: the <strong>character</strong> 4, not the number 4.</li>\n<li><code>a=s[4]</code> has a variable on the right side. That is fine: whatever value is on the right is stored in the variable on the left (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><code>a+b</code> is <code>'4'+'7'</code>: two strings, so <code>+</code> <strong>concatenates</strong> them into <code>'47'</code>. It does not add (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To add, convert each character to an integer first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0123456789&#x27;</span>\na=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>])\nb=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(b)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(a+b)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>4\n7\n11\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are integers, so <code>+</code> adds: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span></span></span></span>.</span> The lines <code>4</code> and <code>7</code> look the same in both outputs; only the type behind them is different.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The moral of the story</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>How Python treats a value depends on how it is stored, that is, on its type. If you need numbers, convert to numbers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). The lecture ends with a puzzle on exactly this; it is in <strong>Check yourself</strong> below.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"more-on-strings\">More on strings</h2>\n<h3 id=\"replication\">Replication</h3>\n<p>Multiplying a string by an integer repeats it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;good&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s * <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>goodgoodgoodgoodgood\n</code></pre>\n<p>Change the second line to <code>print(<wbr>s[0] * 5)</code> and it prints <code>ggggg</code>, because <code>s[0]</code> is <code>'g'</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Replication</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Replication</strong> is repeating a string by multiplying it by an integer: <code>s * 5</code> is <code>s</code> concatenated with itself 5 times. It works for a single character too: <code>'g' * 5</code> is <code>'ggggg'</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So <code>*</code> between a string and an integer is allowed; between two strings it is not (the <code>TypeError</code> in lecture 1.9).</p>\n<h3 id=\"comparing-strings\">Comparing strings</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">x = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;India&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(x == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;India&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(x == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;india&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>True\nFalse\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>==</code> compares strings exactly. A capital <code>I</code> and a small <code>i</code> are different characters, so <code>'India' == 'india'</code> is <code>False</code>: one changed letter is enough (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<p><code>&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;</code> work on strings too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;apple&#x27;</span> &gt; <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;one&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;four&#x27;</span> &lt; <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ten&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>False\nTrue\n</code></pre>\n<p>Strings are not compared by length or by the number they name. Python compares them the way a dictionary orders words: <strong>character by character, from the left</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>'apple' &gt; 'one'</code>: compare the first characters, <code>a</code> and <code>o</code>. In alphabetical order <code>a</code> comes before <code>o</code>, so <code>'apple'</code> is the smaller string and <code>'apple' &gt; 'one'</code> is <code>False</code>, even though <code>'apple'</code> is longer.</li>\n<li><code>'four' &lt; 'ten'</code>: <code>f</code> comes before <code>t</code>, so <code>True</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the first characters are equal, Python moves on to the next pair (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\"><span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ab&#x27;</span> &lt; <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;az&#x27;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;abcdef&#x27;</span> &lt; <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;abcde&#x27;</span>)\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>True\nFalse\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>'ab' &lt; 'az'</code>: <code>a</code> and <code>a</code> are equal; then <code>b</code> comes before <code>z</code>, so <code>True</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Comparing <code>'abcdef'</code> with <code>'abcde'</code> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Index 0: <code>a</code> and <code>a</code> are equal, so move on.</li>\n<li>Indexes 1 to 4: <code>b</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>d</code>, <code>e</code> match <code>b</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>d</code>, <code>e</code>, so keep moving on.</li>\n<li>Index 5: the left string has <code>f</code>, but the right string has ended; there is nothing to compare <code>f</code> with.</li>\n<li>A string that ends first is the smaller one: <code>f</code> “cannot be less than nothing”. So <code>'abcde'</code> is smaller than <code>'abcdef'</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>'abcdef' &lt; 'abcde'</code> is <code>False</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Capital letters (not in the lecture)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Python really compares characters by their code numbers. For small letters this is the usual alphabetical order, as in all the lecture’s examples. But every capital letter comes before every small letter: <code>'Z' &lt; 'a'</code> is <code>True</code>, and so is <code>'India' &lt; 'india'</code>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"negative-indexing\">Negative indexing</h3>\n<p>A string can also be indexed from the end, with negative numbers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;python&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>])\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>n\no\nh\nt\ny\np\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Character</th>\n<th>p</th>\n<th>y</th>\n<th>t</th>\n<th>h</th>\n<th>o</th>\n<th>n</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Index</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative index</td>\n<td>−6</td>\n<td>−5</td>\n<td>−4</td>\n<td>−3</td>\n<td>−2</td>\n<td>−1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code><span>s[-1]</span></code> is the last character, <code><span>s[-2]</span></code> the second last, and so on; <code><span>s[-6]</span></code> is the first character, <code>p</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"index-out-of-range-and-len\">Index out of range, and <code>len()</code></h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;hjvbdhjvbshjcbsjkcjn&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>])\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;main.py&quot;, line 2, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(s[100])\nIndexError: string index out of range\n</code></pre>\n<p>The string of random letters is not long enough to have a character at index 100 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). <strong>Out of range</strong> means outside the allowed indexes. Even a longer string fails, so how do you know the allowed indexes? Count the characters with <code>len</code> (short for <em>length</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;hjvbdhjvbshjcbsjkcjnvbdvbmnbnmdvnmdf&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(s))\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>36\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>print(s[36])</code> still gives <code>IndexError: string index out of range</code> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). The 36 characters have indexes 0, 1, …, 35, so there is no index 36.</li>\n<li><code>print(s[35])</code> prints <code>f</code>, the last character (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e45MVXwya7A&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box fm\" id=\"f3\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Formula</span><span class=\"box-t\">Valid string indexes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><div class=\"md\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:3em;vertical-align:-1.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mtable\"><span class=\"col-align-r\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.75em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.91em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.41em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">or </span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.25em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"col-align-l\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.75em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.91em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">len</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.41em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">len</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.25em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\nwhere <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span></span></span></span></span> is a string and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span></span> an index. The last character is <code><span>s[len(s)-1]</span></code>, which is also <code><span>s[-1]</span></code>; index <code>len(s)</code> is always out of range.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"tips-for-beginners\">Tips for beginners</h2>\n<p>Lecture 1.13 answers questions that beginners often ask (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>“I cannot remember the syntax.”</strong> Nobody knows every word of their mother tongue either, yet they speak it every day. Learn the common parts, look the rest up on the internet, and keep a <strong>cheat sheet</strong> of the syntax you use often. With time you will remember it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“I get errors, and they irritate me.”</strong> Everyone gets errors, even experts. Syntax errors, such as a missing bracket, are very common, and Python usually tells you where the error is so you can fix it at once. “A programmer’s life involves fixing the errors more than writing the code” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Why Python and not another language?”</strong> It is easy for a first-time programmer, powerful and much sought after. Most open-source software is in Python, so millions of projects and library functions are ready to use. And once you know one language well, a second one is like a second spoken language: mostly new syntax for logic you already know (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“How do I get better at coding?”</strong> Practice, as with table tennis, chess or badminton (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). Ask other questions on the course forum.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write the same program again</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>After you solve a problem, do not move on at once. Open a fresh editor and type the solution again without looking, three or four times (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). Coding has two difficulties: <strong>syntax</strong>, which is memory and comes with time, and <strong>logic</strong>, which is thinking and takes longer. Repeating a program makes the syntax automatic, so your mind is free for the logic (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). Choose programs that are a little long; repeating a two-line program does not help (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Ccezy5hlc8&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"error-messages-of-this-week\">Error messages of this week</h2>\n<p>Every error you met this week: the last line as Python 3.12 prints it, what it means, and the fix.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SyntaxError: invalid syntax</code>: the code breaks a writing rule, for example <code>print&lt;'Hi'&gt;</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> check the brackets, quotes and commas in the marked line and in the line above it.</li>\n<li><code>SyntaxError: '(<wbr>' was never closed</code>: a <code>(</code> has no matching <code>)</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> add the missing <code>)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>SyntaxError: unterminated string literal</code>: a string has no closing quote, or mixes <code>&quot;</code> and <code>'</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> end the string with the same quote it starts with.</li>\n<li><code>SyntaxError: cannot assign to literal</code>: a literal stands on the left of <code>=</code>, as in <code>30 = 30 + 1</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> put a variable on the left.</li>\n<li><code>NameError: name 'p' is not defined</code>: the name has no value. It was never assigned, it is misspelled (<code>prnt</code>, <code>true</code>), or it is text without quotes. <strong>Fix:</strong> assign it first, correct the spelling, or add the quotes.</li>\n<li><code>TypeError: can't multiply sequence by <span>non-int</span> of type 'str'</code>: <code>*</code> between two strings. <strong>Fix:</strong> multiply a string only by an integer.</li>\n<li><code>TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for -: 'str' and 'str'</code>: <code>-</code> between two strings. <strong>Fix:</strong> convert to numbers first if you want arithmetic.</li>\n<li><code>TypeError: 'builtin_<wbr>function_<wbr>or_<wbr>method' object is not subscriptable</code>: square brackets after a function, as in <code>print['Hi']</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> use round brackets, <code>print('Hi')</code>.</li>\n<li><code>ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'</code>: <code>int()</code> got text that is not a whole number. <strong>Fix:</strong> give it a whole number.</li>\n<li><code>IndexError: string index out of range</code>: the index is <code>len(s)</code> or more, or below <code><span>-len(s)</span></code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> use indexes from <code>0</code> to <code><span>len(s)-1</span></code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Older wording in the videos</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Python 3.8, used in the videos, words some syntax errors differently: a missing <code>)</code> gives <code>invalid syntax</code> (often on the next line) or <code>unexpected EOF while parsing</code>; mixed quotes give <code>EOL while scanning string literal</code>; and <code>30 = 30 + 1</code> gives <code>cannot assign to literal</code>. The error <strong>type</strong> at the start of the last line is the same.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Programs</strong> run from the first line to the last. <code>print(...)</code> shows values; commas separate several values, which are printed on one line with one space between them.</li>\n<li><strong>Syntax</strong>: round brackets after <code>print</code>; text inside matching quotes (<code>'...'</code> or <code>&quot;...&quot;</code>). A <code>SyntaxError</code> stops the program before it runs; other errors stop it at the bad line. Read error messages from the last line up.</li>\n<li><strong>Variables</strong>: <code>x = value</code> stores the right side in the name on the left. <code>a = a + 1</code> uses the old value to make the new one (incrementing). A literal (<code>10</code>, <code>3.14</code>, <code>'India'</code>) may stand only on the right of <code>=</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Input</strong>: <code>input()</code> waits and returns a string; <code>int(input())</code> gives an integer; <code>input(<wbr>'message')</code> shows a prompt first.</li>\n<li><strong>Data types</strong>: <code>int</code> (<code>10</code>), <code>float</code> (<code>6.3</code>), <code>str</code> (<code>'India'</code>), <code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>, <code>False</code>), <code>list</code> (<code>[10,20,30]</code>); <code>type(x)</code> shows the type.</li>\n<li><strong>Conversion</strong>: <code>int(5.7)</code> is 5 (the decimal part is dropped), <code>int('10')</code> is 10, <code>float(9)</code> is 9.0, <code>str(9)</code> is <code>'9'</code>; <code>bool(x)</code> is <code>False</code> only for <code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code> and <code>''</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Arithmetic</strong>: <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code> (always a float), <code>//</code> (quotient), <code>%</code> (remainder), <code>**</code> (power); <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">q</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span></span></span></span>.</span> <code>*</code> is done before <code>+</code>; brackets first. Floats can show tiny errors: <code>3*2.6</code> prints <code>7.800000000000001</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Relational</strong>: <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code> give <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>. <code>=</code> stores, <code>==</code> compares.</li>\n<li><strong>Logical</strong>: <code>and</code> (both <code>True</code>), <code>or</code> (at least one <code>True</code>), <code>not</code> (flips).</li>\n<li><strong>Strings</strong>: <code>+</code> joins, <code>*</code> with an integer repeats; index from 0 (<code>s[0]</code>) or from the end (<code><span>s[-1]</span></code>); the slice <code>s[a:b]</code> takes indexes <code>a</code> to <code><span>b-1</span></code>; <code>len(s)</code> counts characters, and valid indexes run from 0 to <code><span>len(s)-1</span></code>; comparison is character by character, in dictionary order, and capitals matter.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<p>The lecture’s own exercise from lecture 1.7 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8n4MBjuDBu4&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">l=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">68</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">732</span>]\n\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>(l[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]))\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What do the two print lines of this program show, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Counting starts at 0: <code>l[0]</code> is 10, <code>l[1]</code> is 20, so <code>l[2]</code> is the <strong>third</strong> value, 30. The first line prints <code>30</code>.</li>\n<li><code>l[2]</code> is the number 30, an integer. Each value inside a list keeps its own type, so <code>type(l[2])</code> is <code>int</code>, not <code>list</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>30</code>, then <code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<p>The lecture’s closing puzzle from lecture 1.11 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sS89tiDuqoM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code class=\"hl\">s=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0123456789&#x27;</span>\na=s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>]\nb=s[<span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>]\nn=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>(a+b)\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(n)\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does this print? And what if the conversion moves to the characters: <code>a=int(s[3])</code>, <code>b=int(s[8])</code>, <code>n=a+b</code>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>First program:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>a</code> is the character <code>'3'</code> and <code>b</code> is the character <code>'8'</code>: both strings.</li>\n<li><code>a+b</code> concatenates them: <code>'3'+'8'</code> is <code>'38'</code>.</li>\n<li><code>int('38')</code> converts the text to the number 38.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Second program:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>a=int(s[3])</code> stores the number 3, and <code>b=int(s[8])</code> stores the number 8.</li>\n<li><code>a+b</code> adds them: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the first program prints <code>38</code>, the second prints <code>11</code>. Where you convert decides whether <code>+</code> joins or adds.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does <code>print(<wbr>int(5.7) + float(<wbr>'5.3'))</code> print?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>int(5.7)</code> drops the decimal part: 5.</li>\n<li><code>float('5.3')</code> turns the text into the number 5.3.</li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5.3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10.3</span></span></span></span>.</span> An integer plus a float gives a float.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>10.3</code></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does <code>print(<wbr>'cat' &lt; 'car')</code> print?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Index 0: <code>c</code> and <code>c</code> are equal, so move on.</li>\n<li>Index 1: <code>a</code> and <code>a</code> are equal, so move on.</li>\n<li>Index 2: <code>t</code> and <code>r</code>. In alphabetical order <code>r</code> comes before <code>t</code>, so <code>'car'</code> is the smaller string.</li>\n<li>So “<code>'cat'</code> is less than <code>'car'</code>” is not true.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>False</code></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does <code>print(<wbr>bool('0'), bool(0), bool(''))</code> print?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>'0'</code> is in quotes: a string with one character. A non-empty string gives <code>True</code>.</li>\n<li><code>0</code> is the number zero, which gives <code>False</code>.</li>\n<li><code>''</code> is the empty string, which gives <code>False</code>.</li>\n<li><code>print</code> puts one space between the three values.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>True False False</code></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">With <code>s = 'python'</code>, what does <code>print(<wbr>s[1:4], <span>s[-1])</span></code> print?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><code>s[1:4]</code> takes indexes 1, 2 and 3, stopping before 4: <code>y</code>, <code>t</code>, <code>h</code>, so <code>yth</code>.</li>\n<li><code><span>s[-1]</span></code> is the last character: <code>n</code>.</li>\n<li><code>print</code> puts one space between the two values.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>yth n</code></p>\n</div></details>\n"});
